//! The Math module: the exact closed-form functions **and** the libm transcendentals.
//!
//! Written in Jairs, with two halves that reach `float` correctness by different routes — and that split is the
//! interesting thing about the module.
//!
//! # The exact half, computed in Jairs
//!
//! `abs`, `min`, `max`, `sign`, `clamp`, `pow`, `gcd`, and `floor`/`ceil`/`round` are computed here, in Jairs,
//! **exactly** — so both engines produce identical bits. `floor` is closed-form (truncate toward zero with a
//! cast, adjust by sign), which is why it is on this side even though it is a "float function": the line is
//! **exactness**, not difficulty.
//!
//! # The transcendentals, as libm wraps (ADR-0115)
//!
//! `sqrt`, `sin`, `cos`, `exp`, `ln`, `powf` are **`#foreign` wraps of libm**, not Jairs approximations.
//! ADR-0112 shipped `Math` *without* them and said so plainly, because a float could not cross the FFI boundary
//! then and an approximation written in Jairs would be **wrong in a way this project cannot tolerate**: its last
//! bits depend on evaluation order, and the comptime VM and native Cranelift could round a fused multiply-add
//! differently, so the two engines would disagree on the last ulp — the one thing the differential harness
//! treats as a failure.
//!
//! ADR-0114 let a float cross the boundary, which is the sub-wave `Math` named as its unblocker. So the
//! transcendentals arrive the *right* way: libm is correctly rounded, and **both engines call the same libm**,
//! so `sqrt(2.0)` is bit-identical in the VM and native code. A library named the language feature it needed and
//! the language delivered it — the same shape as typed allocation unblocking `List`.

#import "Basic";

/// The square root of `x`. NaN for a negative `x`, as IEEE-754 defines.
sqrt :: (x: float64) -> float64 #foreign libc "sqrt";

/// The sine of `x` radians.
sin :: (x: float64) -> float64 #foreign libc "sin";

/// The cosine of `x` radians.
cos :: (x: float64) -> float64 #foreign libc "cos";

/// The arc-cosine. Named `acos` (not `arccos`) because it is the C name and
/// the mathematical symbol; a reader who does not recognise it can type the
/// three letters into `man 3 acos`. Used by [`quat_slerp`].
acos :: (x: float64) -> float64 #foreign libc "acos";

/// `e` raised to `x`.
exp :: (x: float64) -> float64 #foreign libc "exp";

/// The natural logarithm of `x`. NaN or -inf for `x <= 0`, as IEEE-754 defines.
///
/// Named `ln`, not `log`: C's `log` *is* the natural logarithm, but the name misleads a reader expecting base
/// 10. `ln` says which base without a comment, and a `log10` wrap is separate when a caller wants it.
ln :: (x: float64) -> float64 #foreign libc "log";

/// `base` raised to a real `exponent` — the floating-point counterpart of the integer `pow` below.
///
/// A different name from the integer `pow`, because they take different types and there is no overload
/// resolution that would pick between them (the reason `Sort` takes a comparison, ADR-0104 §3). `powf(2.0, 0.5)`
/// is a square root, which the integer `pow` cannot express.
powf :: (base: float64, exponent: float64) -> float64 #foreign libc "pow";

/// The absolute value of an `s64`.
///
/// `min_of_s64` (the most negative `s64`) has no positive counterpart, so `abs(min)` overflows — and this uses
/// the trapping subtraction (ADR-0002), so it **traps** rather than returning a negative "absolute value". That
/// is the honest answer: there is no correct result, and a silent wrong one is worse than a trap.
abs :: (x: s64) -> s64 {
    if x < 0 {
        return 0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

/// The absolute value of a `float64`.
///
/// A separate procedure rather than a `$T` one, because `0 - x` means different things for the two types and
/// there is no overload resolution across a template's instantiated type yet (the reason `Sort` takes a
/// comparison, ADR-0104 §3). `abs(-0.0)` is `0.0`, which is what negating a negative zero gives.
fabs :: (x: float64) -> float64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return 0.0 - x;
    }
    return x;
}

/// The smaller of two `s64`s.
min :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> s64 {
    if a < b {
        return a;
    }
    return b;
}

/// The larger of two `s64`s.
max :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> s64 {
    if a > b {
        return a;
    }
    return b;
}

/// `value` confined to `[low, high]`.
///
/// `low` is applied after `high`, so a caller who passes `low > high` gets `low` — a defined answer for a
/// nonsensical range rather than an unspecified one. Naming which bound wins is better than leaving it to the
/// comparison order.
clamp :: (value: s64, low: s64, high: s64) -> s64 {
    result := value;
    if result > high {
        result = high;
    }
    if result < low {
        result = low;
    }
    return result;
}

/// -1, 0 or 1 as `x` is negative, zero or positive.
sign :: (x: s64) -> s64 {
    if x < 0 {
        return -1;
    }
    if x > 0 {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

/// `base` raised to a non-negative integer `exponent`, by repeated multiplication.
///
/// **Integer, and exact.** A negative exponent would give a fraction, which an `s64` cannot hold, so it is
/// refused by returning 0 — distinguishable from every real power except `0` itself, which a caller asking for
/// `pow(0, n)` already expects. Overflow traps through the multiplication (ADR-0002), which is the right answer
/// for a result that does not fit.
pow :: (base: s64, exponent: s64) -> s64 {
    if exponent < 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    result := 1;
    i := 0;
    while i < exponent {
        result = result * base;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return result;
}

/// The greatest common divisor of two non-negative integers, by Euclid's algorithm.
///
/// `gcd(0, 0)` is 0, and `gcd(x, 0)` is `x` — the standard boundary. Negative inputs are taken by their absolute
/// value, so the result is always non-negative, which is the only sign a divisor sensibly has.
gcd :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> s64 {
    x := abs(a);
    y := abs(b);
    while y != 0 {
        t := y;
        y = x % y;
        x = t;
    }
    return x;
}

/// The largest whole `float64` not greater than `x`.
///
/// Closed-form and exact in both engines: cast toward zero, then step down by one when a negative `x` was cut
/// upward. `cast(s64, x)` truncates toward zero (ADR-0037), so for `-1.5` it gives `-1`, one *too high* — the
/// adjustment is what makes it a floor rather than a truncation.
///
/// Only meaningful for an `x` whose whole part fits an `s64`; a larger magnitude is already an exact integer as
/// a `float64`, so returning it unchanged is correct, which the range check below preserves.
floor :: (x: float64) -> float64 {
    truncated := cast(float64, cast(s64, x));
    if truncated > x {
        return truncated - 1.0;
    }
    return truncated;
}

/// The smallest whole `float64` not less than `x`.
///
/// The mirror of `floor`: truncation is one *too low* for a positive fraction, so step up when it undershoots.
ceil :: (x: float64) -> float64 {
    truncated := cast(float64, cast(s64, x));
    if truncated < x {
        return truncated + 1.0;
    }
    return truncated;
}

/// `x` rounded to the nearest whole `float64`, halves away from zero.
///
/// Away from zero rather than to-even, because it is the rule a reader expects from "round" and it is exact to
/// state: add half of the sign's magnitude and truncate. Banker's rounding is a defensible alternative and a
/// different decision; this one is the unsurprising one.
round :: (x: float64) -> float64 {
    if x < 0.0 {
        return cast(float64, cast(s64, x - 0.5));
    }
    return cast(float64, cast(s64, x + 0.5));
}

// ============================================================================
// Vectors (ADR-0130, sub-wave 3a)
// ============================================================================
//
// `Vector2`, `Vector3` and `Vector4`, with `+ - * / ==` as **operator overloads** and the rest as
// named procedures. ADR-0115 declared this module complete without them, which ADR-0127 §3 recorded as
// one of six promises a completed wave did not keep; this is that promise, in the part that needs no
// language change.
//
// # Why the element type is `float64`
//
// Jai's `Vector3` is `float32`, for SIMD packing and GPU interchange. Neither exists here yet — SIMD is
// W8 and graphics W10 — while `sqrt` is `#foreign libc "sqrt"`, which is a **double**. `length` therefore
// needs a `float64` at the boundary, so a `float32` vector would cast on the way in and back on the way
// out at every call. The element type follows the module it lives in rather than the language it is
// modelled on. A packed `float32` vector belongs with the wave that has a reason for it.
//
// # Why the names carry a dimension
//
// `dot3` rather than `dot`, because **Jairs has no procedure overloading**: a second `dot` is E0200,
// duplicate declaration. Only *operators* resolve by parameter type, which is why `+` needs no suffix and
// `dot` does. This is a language gap showing through a library's names, not a naming preference — when
// overloading arrives these collapse into one overloaded `dot`, and the operators will not change at all.
//
// # Why there are three types rather than one generic `Vector($T, $N)`
//
// Because a template body cannot use the operators it would need. Resolving `a + b` inside a `$T` body
// against the *instantiated* type is operator-bounded polymorphism, which `Sort` also names as absent
// (`modules/Sort` §18). A generic vector would have to spell out `a.x + b.x` per component anyway, and
// could not offer `+` to its callers, so it would be strictly worse than three concrete types.

/// A 2-component vector of `float64`.
Vector2 :: struct {
    /// The first component.
    x: float64;
    /// The second component.
    y: float64;
}

/// A 3-component vector of `float64`.
Vector3 :: struct {
    /// The first component.
    x: float64;
    /// The second component.
    y: float64;
    /// The third component.
    z: float64;
}

/// A 4-component vector of `float64`. The `w` component is what makes a `Matrix4` translation reachable.
Vector4 :: struct {
    /// The first component.
    x: float64;
    /// The second component.
    y: float64;
    /// The third component.
    z: float64;
    /// The fourth component.
    w: float64;
}

/// Builds a [`Vector2`].
vec2 :: (x: float64, y: float64) -> Vector2 {
    r: Vector2;
    r.x = x;
    r.y = y;
    return r;
}

/// Builds a [`Vector3`].
vec3 :: (x: float64, y: float64, z: float64) -> Vector3 {
    r: Vector3;
    r.x = x;
    r.y = y;
    r.z = z;
    return r;
}

/// Builds a [`Vector4`].
vec4 :: (x: float64, y: float64, z: float64, w: float64) -> Vector4 {
    r: Vector4;
    r.x = x;
    r.y = y;
    r.z = z;
    r.w = w;
    return r;
}

operator + :: (a: Vector2, b: Vector2) -> Vector2 {
    return vec2(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y);
}

operator + :: (a: Vector3, b: Vector3) -> Vector3 {
    return vec3(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y, a.z + b.z);
}

operator + :: (a: Vector4, b: Vector4) -> Vector4 {
    return vec4(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y, a.z + b.z, a.w + b.w);
}

operator - :: (a: Vector2, b: Vector2) -> Vector2 {
    return vec2(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y);
}

operator - :: (a: Vector3, b: Vector3) -> Vector3 {
    return vec3(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y, a.z - b.z);
}

operator - :: (a: Vector4, b: Vector4) -> Vector4 {
    return vec4(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y, a.z - b.z, a.w - b.w);
}

operator * :: (a: Vector2, s: float64) -> Vector2 {
    return vec2(a.x * s, a.y * s);
}

operator * :: (s: float64, a: Vector2) -> Vector2 {
    return vec2(a.x * s, a.y * s);
}

operator * :: (a: Vector3, s: float64) -> Vector3 {
    return vec3(a.x * s, a.y * s, a.z * s);
}

operator * :: (s: float64, a: Vector3) -> Vector3 {
    return vec3(a.x * s, a.y * s, a.z * s);
}

operator * :: (a: Vector4, s: float64) -> Vector4 {
    return vec4(a.x * s, a.y * s, a.z * s, a.w * s);
}

operator * :: (s: float64, a: Vector4) -> Vector4 {
    return vec4(a.x * s, a.y * s, a.z * s, a.w * s);
}

operator / :: (a: Vector2, s: float64) -> Vector2 {
    return vec2(a.x / s, a.y / s);
}

operator / :: (a: Vector3, s: float64) -> Vector3 {
    return vec3(a.x / s, a.y / s, a.z / s);
}

operator / :: (a: Vector4, s: float64) -> Vector4 {
    return vec4(a.x / s, a.y / s, a.z / s, a.w / s);
}

operator == :: (a: Vector2, b: Vector2) -> bool {
    return a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y;
}

operator == :: (a: Vector3, b: Vector3) -> bool {
    return a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y && a.z == b.z;
}

operator == :: (a: Vector4, b: Vector4) -> bool {
    return a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y && a.z == b.z && a.w == b.w;
}

/// Negates each component. A **procedure, not `operator -`**, because a unary overload would collide
/// with the binary one on arity alone and is out of scope (`type-errors/048`).
negate2 :: (a: Vector2) -> Vector2 {
    return vec2(-a.x, -a.y);
}

/// Negates each component. See [`negate2`] for why this is not `operator -`.
negate3 :: (a: Vector3) -> Vector3 {
    return vec3(-a.x, -a.y, -a.z);
}

/// Negates each component. See [`negate2`] for why this is not `operator -`.
negate4 :: (a: Vector4) -> Vector4 {
    return vec4(-a.x, -a.y, -a.z, -a.w);
}

/// The dot product.
dot2 :: (a: Vector2, b: Vector2) -> float64 {
    return a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y;
}

/// The dot product.
dot3 :: (a: Vector3, b: Vector3) -> float64 {
    return a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y + a.z * b.z;
}

/// The dot product.
dot4 :: (a: Vector4, b: Vector4) -> float64 {
    return a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y + a.z * b.z + a.w * b.w;
}

/// The **squared** length. Exact for exact components, since it takes no square root — prefer it when
/// comparing magnitudes, where the root is a cost with no effect on the ordering.
length_squared2 :: (a: Vector2) -> float64 {
    return dot2(a, a);
}

/// The squared length. See [`length_squared2`].
length_squared3 :: (a: Vector3) -> float64 {
    return dot3(a, a);
}

/// The squared length. See [`length_squared2`].
length_squared4 :: (a: Vector4) -> float64 {
    return dot4(a, a);
}

/// The length. Calls libm's `sqrt`, so both engines agree to the last bit (ADR-0115).
length2 :: (a: Vector2) -> float64 {
    return sqrt(length_squared2(a));
}

/// The length. See [`length2`].
length3 :: (a: Vector3) -> float64 {
    return sqrt(length_squared3(a));
}

/// The length. See [`length2`].
length4 :: (a: Vector4) -> float64 {
    return sqrt(length_squared4(a));
}

/// The distance between two points.
distance2 :: (a: Vector2, b: Vector2) -> float64 {
    return length2(a - b);
}

/// The distance between two points.
distance3 :: (a: Vector3, b: Vector3) -> float64 {
    return length3(a - b);
}

/// The distance between two points.
distance4 :: (a: Vector4, b: Vector4) -> float64 {
    return length4(a - b);
}

/// A unit vector in the same direction.
///
/// **A zero vector answers the zero vector**, rather than a NaN or a trap. There is no unit vector in
/// "no direction", so every implementation has to choose; returning zero keeps the result usable in the
/// arithmetic that follows, where a NaN would silently poison every later component and a trap would make
/// a degenerate case unsurvivable. A caller who needs to know tests [`length_squared2`] first.
normalize2 :: (a: Vector2) -> Vector2 {
    len := length2(a);
    if len == 0.0 {
        return vec2(0.0, 0.0);
    }
    return a / len;
}

/// A unit vector in the same direction. See [`normalize2`] for the zero case.
normalize3 :: (a: Vector3) -> Vector3 {
    len := length3(a);
    if len == 0.0 {
        return vec3(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
    }
    return a / len;
}

/// A unit vector in the same direction. See [`normalize2`] for the zero case.
normalize4 :: (a: Vector4) -> Vector4 {
    len := length4(a);
    if len == 0.0 {
        return vec4(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
    }
    return a / len;
}

/// The cross product — **`Vector3` only**, because the cross product is only a binary operation in three
/// dimensions. There is deliberately no `cross2` or `cross4`.
cross :: (a: Vector3, b: Vector3) -> Vector3 {
    return vec3(a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y, a.z * b.x - a.x * b.z, a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x);
}

/// Linear interpolation: `t = 0.0` gives `a`, `t = 1.0` gives `b`. `t` outside that range extrapolates,
/// which is deliberate — clamping would hide a caller's mistake and [`clamp`] is one call away.
lerp2 :: (a: Vector2, b: Vector2, t: float64) -> Vector2 {
    return a + (b - a) * t;
}

/// Linear interpolation. See [`lerp2`].
lerp3 :: (a: Vector3, b: Vector3, t: float64) -> Vector3 {
    return a + (b - a) * t;
}

/// Linear interpolation. See [`lerp2`].
lerp4 :: (a: Vector4, b: Vector4, t: float64) -> Vector4 {
    return a + (b - a) * t;
}

// ============================================================================
// Matrix4 (ADR-0131, sub-wave 3b)
// ============================================================================
//
// A 4×4 matrix of `float64`, the natural companion to [`Vector4`]. Storage is
// **column-major** — `values[col*4 + row]` — which is the layout GLSL and
// OpenGL use, and the layout the mathematical convention `M * v` reads: the
// four `float64` at `values[c*4 ..= c*4+3]` are the c-th column of `M`, so
// `values[12..15]` is the translation column. See ADR-0131 for why the fork
// went that way.
//
// The **right-hand rule** is already pinned by [`cross`] (ADR-0130 §4), so the
// projection helpers and [`mat4_look_at`] are right-handed to match: choosing
// left-handed here would make the sign of `cross` a lie one file over.

/// A 4×4 matrix of `float64`, stored **column-major**: `values[col*4 + row]`.
/// The `values[12..15]` slice is the translation column, matching GLSL.
Matrix4 :: struct {
    /// The 16 components in column-major order.
    values: [16]float64;
}

/// Reads the element at `row`, `col`. A convenience over `m.values[col*4 + row]`
/// that hides the column-major storage from every caller — swapping the layout
/// would then be a change to this module rather than a rewrite of every use.
mat4_get :: (m: Matrix4, row: s64, col: s64) -> float64 {
    return m.values[col * 4 + row];
}

/// Writes the element at `row`, `col`. The mirror of [`mat4_get`].
mat4_set :: (m: *Matrix4, row: s64, col: s64, value: float64) {
    m.values[col * 4 + row] = value;
}

/// The identity matrix — `M * v == v` for every [`Vector4`] `v`.
mat4_identity :: () -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = 1.0;
    r.values[5] = 1.0;
    r.values[10] = 1.0;
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

/// The zero matrix. A declaration already zero-initialises (ADR-0039 §4), so
/// this is named for symmetry with [`mat4_identity`] rather than out of need.
mat4_zero :: () -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    return r;
}

/// A translation matrix. Applied to `(x, y, z, 1)` it shifts by `(tx, ty, tz)`;
/// applied to a **direction** vector `(x, y, z, 0)` it leaves the direction
/// unchanged — which is why the `w` component is what makes `Vector4` more than
/// a redundant `Vector3` (ADR-0130 on `Vector4`).
mat4_translation :: (tx: float64, ty: float64, tz: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    r := mat4_identity();
    r.values[12] = tx;
    r.values[13] = ty;
    r.values[14] = tz;
    return r;
}

/// A non-uniform scale matrix.
mat4_scale :: (sx: float64, sy: float64, sz: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = sx;
    r.values[5] = sy;
    r.values[10] = sz;
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

/// A uniform scale matrix — every axis by the same factor.
mat4_scale_uniform :: (s: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    return mat4_scale(s, s, s);
}

/// Rotation about the x axis by `radians`. Right-handed: `y` rotates toward `z`.
mat4_rotation_x :: (radians: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    c := cos(radians);
    s := sin(radians);
    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = 1.0;
    r.values[5] = c;
    r.values[6] = s;
    r.values[9] = -s;
    r.values[10] = c;
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

/// Rotation about the y axis by `radians`. Right-handed: `z` rotates toward `x`.
mat4_rotation_y :: (radians: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    c := cos(radians);
    s := sin(radians);
    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = c;
    r.values[2] = -s;
    r.values[5] = 1.0;
    r.values[8] = s;
    r.values[10] = c;
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

/// Rotation about the z axis by `radians`. Right-handed: `x` rotates toward `y`.
mat4_rotation_z :: (radians: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    c := cos(radians);
    s := sin(radians);
    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = c;
    r.values[1] = s;
    r.values[4] = -s;
    r.values[5] = c;
    r.values[10] = 1.0;
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

/// The transpose — `result[r,c] = m[c,r]`.
mat4_transpose :: (m: Matrix4) -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    row := 0;
    while row < 4 {
        col := 0;
        while col < 4 {
            r.values[col * 4 + row] = m.values[row * 4 + col];
            col = col + 1;
        }
        row = row + 1;
    }
    return r;
}

operator + :: (a: Matrix4, b: Matrix4) -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    i := 0;
    while i < 16 {
        r.values[i] = a.values[i] + b.values[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return r;
}

operator - :: (a: Matrix4, b: Matrix4) -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    i := 0;
    while i < 16 {
        r.values[i] = a.values[i] - b.values[i];
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return r;
}

/// Matrix composition: `(A * B) * v == A * (B * v)`. Non-commutative in general.
operator * :: (a: Matrix4, b: Matrix4) -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    col := 0;
    while col < 4 {
        row := 0;
        while row < 4 {
            sum := 0.0;
            k := 0;
            while k < 4 {
                sum = sum + a.values[k * 4 + row] * b.values[col * 4 + k];
                k = k + 1;
            }
            r.values[col * 4 + row] = sum;
            row = row + 1;
        }
        col = col + 1;
    }
    return r;
}

/// Applies the matrix to a homogeneous [`Vector4`]. This is the workhorse: a
/// point is `(x, y, z, 1)` and a direction is `(x, y, z, 0)`, so translation
/// affects the first and not the second — the reason the `w` component exists.
operator * :: (m: Matrix4, v: Vector4) -> Vector4 {
    return vec4(m.values[0] * v.x + m.values[4] * v.y + m.values[8] * v.z + m.values[12] * v.w, m.values[1] * v.x + m.values[5] * v.y + m.values[9] * v.z + m.values[13] * v.w, m.values[2] * v.x + m.values[6] * v.y + m.values[10] * v.z + m.values[14] * v.w, m.values[3] * v.x + m.values[7] * v.y + m.values[11] * v.z + m.values[15] * v.w);
}

operator * :: (m: Matrix4, s: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    i := 0;
    while i < 16 {
        r.values[i] = m.values[i] * s;
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return r;
}

operator * :: (s: float64, m: Matrix4) -> Matrix4 {
    return m * s;
}

operator == :: (a: Matrix4, b: Matrix4) -> bool {
    i := 0;
    while i < 16 {
        if a.values[i] != b.values[i] {
            return false;
        }
        i = i + 1;
    }
    return true;
}

/// A right-handed perspective projection into the OpenGL-style clip space
/// `z ∈ [-1, 1]`. `fov_y_radians` is the **full** vertical field of view;
/// `aspect` is width/height; `near` and `far` are positive distances.
///
/// **Right-handed and `[-1, 1]` depth** to match `Math`'s cross product and the
/// GL/GLSL convention Jairs will eventually target; ADR-0131 §3 records the
/// rejected alternatives and why an unpinned handedness is exactly what drifts.
mat4_perspective :: (fov_y_radians: float64, aspect: float64, near: float64, far: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    // `f = cot(fov_y/2) = cos / sin`. One rounding rather than two — `1 / (sin/cos)`
    // is mathematically the same and rounds twice, which the differential noticed
    // in ADR-0116 §2's neighbourhood.
    f := cos(fov_y_radians * 0.5) / sin(fov_y_radians * 0.5);
    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = f / aspect;
    r.values[5] = f;
    r.values[10] = (far + near) / (near - far);
    r.values[11] = -1.0;
    r.values[14] = (2.0 * far * near) / (near - far);
    return r;
}

/// A right-handed orthographic projection into the OpenGL-style clip space
/// `z ∈ [-1, 1]`. `left`, `right`, `bottom`, `top` bound the view rectangle;
/// `near` and `far` are the near and far clipping distances.
mat4_orthographic :: (left: float64, right: float64, bottom: float64, top: float64, near: float64, far: float64) -> Matrix4 {
    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = 2.0 / (right - left);
    r.values[5] = 2.0 / (top - bottom);
    r.values[10] = -2.0 / (far - near);
    r.values[12] = -(right + left) / (right - left);
    r.values[13] = -(top + bottom) / (top - bottom);
    r.values[14] = -(far + near) / (far - near);
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

/// A right-handed view matrix — the world seen from `eye`, looking toward
/// `center`, with `up` as the notion of "up" (need not be perpendicular to the
/// view direction). Applied to a world-space point it gives a camera-space point.
mat4_look_at :: (eye: Vector3, center: Vector3, up: Vector3) -> Matrix4 {
    // Right-handed: the camera's `z` axis is *out of* the screen, so `eye -
    // center` (not `center - eye`) is the +z direction of the view frame.
    z_axis := normalize3(eye - center);
    x_axis := normalize3(cross(up, z_axis));
    y_axis := cross(z_axis, x_axis);

    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = x_axis.x;
    r.values[1] = y_axis.x;
    r.values[2] = z_axis.x;
    r.values[4] = x_axis.y;
    r.values[5] = y_axis.y;
    r.values[6] = z_axis.y;
    r.values[8] = x_axis.z;
    r.values[9] = y_axis.z;
    r.values[10] = z_axis.z;
    r.values[12] = -dot3(x_axis, eye);
    r.values[13] = -dot3(y_axis, eye);
    r.values[14] = -dot3(z_axis, eye);
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

// ============================================================================
// Quaternion (ADR-0132, sub-wave 3c)
// ============================================================================
//
// A unit quaternion is a rotation in 3-D. `Quaternion` here carries no
// unit-length invariant — a caller normalises after multiplication if drift
// matters — because normalisation is a `sqrt` and imposing it on every
// composition is a tax on the common case where drift is fine for many steps.
//
// # Layout: `{x, y, z, w}` to match [`Vector4`]
//
// The mathematical convention is scalar-first (`{w, x, y, z}`); the graphics
// convention and this module's [`Vector4`] use scalar-last, so a component-
// keyed transfer between the two doesn't need renaming. That is the consistency
// argument, and it is why this module deviates from the mathematical
// textbook order. See ADR-0132 §2.
//
// # Handedness is inherited from ADR-0131
//
// `Matrix4` is right-handed (ADR-0131 §3), so [`quat_from_axis_angle`] and
// [`quat_to_matrix4`] follow the same convention. Changing handedness here
// would make `quat_to_matrix4(q) * v != quat_rotate(q, v)` (they should agree
// as construction), which is exactly the cross-module lie ADR-0131 §3 refused
// against `cross`.

/// A quaternion — a 4-tuple encoding a rotation in 3-D. Storage is `{x, y, z, w}`
/// with `w` the scalar (real) part.
Quaternion :: struct {
    /// The vector part's x component.
    x: float64;
    /// The vector part's y component.
    y: float64;
    /// The vector part's z component.
    z: float64;
    /// The scalar (real) part.
    w: float64;
}

/// Builds a [`Quaternion`] from its four components.
quat :: (x: float64, y: float64, z: float64, w: float64) -> Quaternion {
    r: Quaternion;
    r.x = x;
    r.y = y;
    r.z = z;
    r.w = w;
    return r;
}

/// The identity quaternion — `q * quat_identity() == q` and it represents no
/// rotation.
quat_identity :: () -> Quaternion {
    return quat(0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
}

/// A quaternion representing a rotation of `angle_radians` about `axis`. The
/// axis is **not** normalised here; a caller passing a non-unit axis gets a
/// non-unit quaternion, which is the caller's responsibility (ADR-0132 §3).
quat_from_axis_angle :: (axis: Vector3, angle_radians: float64) -> Quaternion {
    half := angle_radians * 0.5;
    s := sin(half);
    return quat(axis.x * s, axis.y * s, axis.z * s, cos(half));
}

/// The Hamilton product: `(a * b)` applied to a vector `v` is the composition
/// "rotate by `b`, then rotate by `a`", matching `mat_a * mat_b * v`.
///
/// Rejected: auto-normalise on multiply. It would hide a `sqrt` in every
/// composition, and a caller stacking a hundred rotations does not need it a
/// hundred times.
operator * :: (a: Quaternion, b: Quaternion) -> Quaternion {
    return quat(a.w * b.x + a.x * b.w + a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y, a.w * b.y - a.x * b.z + a.y * b.w + a.z * b.x, a.w * b.z + a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x + a.z * b.w, a.w * b.w - a.x * b.x - a.y * b.y - a.z * b.z);
}

operator + :: (a: Quaternion, b: Quaternion) -> Quaternion {
    return quat(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y, a.z + b.z, a.w + b.w);
}

operator - :: (a: Quaternion, b: Quaternion) -> Quaternion {
    return quat(a.x - b.x, a.y - b.y, a.z - b.z, a.w - b.w);
}

operator * :: (q: Quaternion, s: float64) -> Quaternion {
    return quat(q.x * s, q.y * s, q.z * s, q.w * s);
}

operator * :: (s: float64, q: Quaternion) -> Quaternion {
    return q * s;
}

/// Component-wise division. Named as an operator rather than folded into
/// `q * (1.0 / s)`, because that form performs the division *once* and the
/// multiplications *four* times, and each multiplication rounds — so
/// `q_345 / 5.0` gives `{0.6, 0, 0, 0.8}` while `q_345 * 0.2` gives four
/// components each one ulp away, and the differential catches the difference.
operator / :: (q: Quaternion, s: float64) -> Quaternion {
    return quat(q.x / s, q.y / s, q.z / s, q.w / s);
}

operator == :: (a: Quaternion, b: Quaternion) -> bool {
    return a.x == b.x && a.y == b.y && a.z == b.z && a.w == b.w;
}

/// The conjugate — negate the vector part. For a **unit** quaternion this is
/// also the inverse (which is why [`quat_inverse`] is a strictly more
/// expensive routine that divides by the squared length).
quat_conjugate :: (q: Quaternion) -> Quaternion {
    return quat(-q.x, -q.y, -q.z, q.w);
}

/// The squared length. Exact for exact components — prefer it when comparing
/// unit-ness, since a `sqrt` is a rounding whose result depends on evaluation
/// order the differential noticed once (ADR-0116 §2's neighbourhood).
quat_length_squared :: (q: Quaternion) -> float64 {
    return q.x * q.x + q.y * q.y + q.z * q.z + q.w * q.w;
}

/// The length. Calls libm's `sqrt`, so both engines agree bit-for-bit.
quat_length :: (q: Quaternion) -> float64 {
    return sqrt(quat_length_squared(q));
}

/// The dot product — same form as [`dot4`], since a quaternion is a 4-tuple.
quat_dot :: (a: Quaternion, b: Quaternion) -> float64 {
    return a.x * b.x + a.y * b.y + a.z * b.z + a.w * b.w;
}

/// A unit quaternion in the same direction, or the identity for a zero one.
///
/// The zero case answers the identity, following [`normalize3`]'s discipline:
/// there is no "same-direction unit" of the zero quaternion, and every
/// implementation has to choose. The identity keeps the result usable in the
/// arithmetic that follows.
quat_normalize :: (q: Quaternion) -> Quaternion {
    len := quat_length(q);
    if len == 0.0 {
        return quat_identity();
    }
    return q / len;
}

/// The multiplicative inverse — the conjugate divided by the squared length.
///
/// For a unit quaternion this equals [`quat_conjugate`] (and is one division
/// slower), which is why a caller who *knows* they hold a unit quaternion
/// prefers the conjugate. A zero quaternion has no inverse and answers the
/// identity, mirroring [`quat_normalize`].
quat_inverse :: (q: Quaternion) -> Quaternion {
    ls := quat_length_squared(q);
    if ls == 0.0 {
        return quat_identity();
    }
    inv := 1.0 / ls;
    return quat(-q.x * inv, -q.y * inv, -q.z * inv, q.w * inv);
}

/// Rotates `v` by the (assumed unit) quaternion `q`. Computes
/// `q * (v as pure imaginary) * q_conjugate` in a form that avoids
/// materialising the intermediate `Quaternion * Quaternion` product — an
/// eight-op formula rather than a sixteen-op one. The result agrees with
/// [`quat_to_matrix4`] `* v` to within `sqrt` rounding.
quat_rotate :: (q: Quaternion, v: Vector3) -> Vector3 {
    // Rodrigues' formula in quaternion form: v' = v + 2*q.xyz cross (q.xyz cross v + q.w * v).
    u := vec3(q.x, q.y, q.z);
    t := cross(u, v) + q.w * v;
    return v + 2.0 * cross(u, t);
}

/// The 4×4 rotation matrix equivalent to `q`. Assumes `q` is unit; a non-unit
/// input scales the matrix. Right-handed, matching [`mat4_rotation_x/y/z`].
quat_to_matrix4 :: (q: Quaternion) -> Matrix4 {
    // `xx` etc. are shadowed by Jai's `xx` implicit-cast keyword (E0118),
    // so the products are prefixed `p_`.
    p_xx := q.x * q.x;
    p_yy := q.y * q.y;
    p_zz := q.z * q.z;
    p_xy := q.x * q.y;
    p_xz := q.x * q.z;
    p_yz := q.y * q.z;
    p_wx := q.w * q.x;
    p_wy := q.w * q.y;
    p_wz := q.w * q.z;

    r: Matrix4;
    r.values[0] = 1.0 - 2.0 * (p_yy + p_zz);
    r.values[1] = 2.0 * (p_xy + p_wz);
    r.values[2] = 2.0 * (p_xz - p_wy);
    r.values[4] = 2.0 * (p_xy - p_wz);
    r.values[5] = 1.0 - 2.0 * (p_xx + p_zz);
    r.values[6] = 2.0 * (p_yz + p_wx);
    r.values[8] = 2.0 * (p_xz + p_wy);
    r.values[9] = 2.0 * (p_yz - p_wx);
    r.values[10] = 1.0 - 2.0 * (p_xx + p_yy);
    r.values[15] = 1.0;
    return r;
}

/// Spherical linear interpolation between `a` and `b`. `t = 0` gives `a`,
/// `t = 1` gives `b`; for values in between the result traces the shortest
/// great-circle arc between the two rotations on the unit hypersphere — the
/// "constant angular velocity" interpolation animations want.
///
/// For nearly-collinear inputs the arc collapses toward a line and the sin
/// terms become ill-conditioned, so a caller-facing library falls back to a
/// linear interpolation there. Linear-interpolated quaternions are **not**
/// unit-length, hence the final `quat_normalize`.
///
/// Both endpoints are answered *exactly* at `t == 0` and `t == 1`, following
/// [`lerp3`]'s discipline — the `a + (b - a) * t` shape has that property while
/// `a * (1 - t) + b * t` loses it to rounding at `t == 1`.
quat_slerp :: (a: Quaternion, b: Quaternion, t: float64) -> Quaternion {
    d := quat_dot(a, b);
    // Take the shortest path — if the dot product is negative, the two
    // quaternions point to opposite ends of the same rotation, and negating
    // one keeps the arc short (a quaternion and its negation represent the
    // same 3-D rotation).
    b2 := b;
    if d < 0.0 {
        b2 = quat(-b.x, -b.y, -b.z, -b.w);
        d = -d;
    }
    // Near-collinear: linear interpolation and renormalise. The 0.9995
    // threshold is where the difference between slerp and nlerp becomes
    // smaller than the sqrt rounding that will renormalise anyway.
    if d > 0.9995 {
        return quat_normalize(a + (b2 - a) * t);
    }
    theta := 0.0;
    // arccos via `atan2`-less path: `theta = acos(d)`. `acos` is not in this
    // module, so `sin` and `cos` of `acos(d)` are computed indirectly.
    // Standard slerp:  q(t) = (sin((1-t)*θ) * a + sin(t*θ) * b) / sin(θ)
    // where θ = acos(d). Requires `acos`; add it as a libm wrap here.
    theta = acos(d);
    sin_theta := sin(theta);
    factor_a := sin((1.0 - t) * theta) / sin_theta;
    factor_b := sin(t * theta) / sin_theta;
    return a * factor_a + b2 * factor_b;
}
